  "editorial_md": "### Intuition\nEvery element makes one independent yes/no decision, so there are 2ⁿ subsets. Backtracking walks that decision tree; an iterative version builds it level by level.\n\n### Backtracking\nAt index `i`, first recurse without `nums[i]`, then push it, recurse, and pop it. When `i == n`, record a **copy** of the current subset.\n\n### Iterative doubling\nStart from `[[]]`. For each number, append it to a copy of every subset collected so far — the collection doubles each time.\n\n```python\ndef subsets(nums):\n    result = [[]]\n    for x in nums:\n        result += [subset + [x] for subset in result]\n    return result\n```\n\n### Complexity\nO(n · 2ⁿ) time and output size.\n\n### Pitfall\nAppending the shared `current` list instead of a copy leaves every recorded subset pointing at the same (eventually empty) list.",
